1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Transcribed by ESO, translated by —

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,940
What about now?

3
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:46,980
Danny gave me the rundown.

4
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:53,220
I just want to start out, this is not in my notes, but I just want to give you guys a testimony.

5
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:54,520
Isn't God good?

6
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,840
I could sit down right now, and Ryan and Miss Donna have preached my sermon.

7
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:04,140
So, I'm really just here to reinforce the fact that the Holy Spirit is already at work in this room.

8
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:07,120
Amen.

9
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:08,580
But good morning, everyone.

10
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:10,380
My name is Corey Smith.

11
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:13,780
For those of you that don't know me, and I have the privilege to be able to get up here once in a while

12
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:16,540
and do some teaching here at Canvas Community Church.

13
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:22,800
So, over the last couple weeks, Ryan and Kent have helped lead us into the story of Jonah.

14
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,780
And Kent gave us something like a telescope.

15
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:28,160
We can see far away.

16
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,920
We kind of get that big picture to see the whole story of Jonah.

17
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,860
Who is a prophet running from God, who God keeps pursuing.

18
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:41,600
A story that we are learning is much bigger than a man and a fish.

19
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,380
Then last week, Ryan gave us more of what I'm going to call a microscope.

20
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:50,480
He helped us zoom in close.

21
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:55,460
He helped us to see Jonah from the depths, Jonah from the belly of the fish, praying.

22
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:58,500
But not praying until after God had already saved him.

23
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:03,140
Being brought face to face with his own rebellion and his own need for mercy.

24
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:07,920
And this morning, as we come to Jonah 3, I want to take us back out again.

25
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:10,560
Kind of that telescope view, but I'm more of a hybrid.

26
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,600
So, I'll be zooming in and out with you guys.

27
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,820
Not because details don't matter.

28
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,460
They do.

29
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:18,380
You know me.

30
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:23,380
But because I believe Jonah 3 forces us to see something bigger.

31
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:29,660
It forces us not to see Jonah only as an individual, but Jonah as a representative of the people of God.

32
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:32,700
Jonah as a mirror.

33
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:39,800
Jonah as a picture of what can happen when the people of God, when we gladly receive God's mercy for ourselves,

34
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,220
but we struggle when that mercy moves outwards towards someone else.

35
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:47,740
So, this morning, before we read Jonah 3, I want to put something in front of us.

36
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:54,120
This is, maybe, Ryan set me up for failure.

37
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:59,900
A mirror.

38
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:01,120
Yes.

39
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:04,900
And Ryan, if you don't mind, Ryan's got some handouts.

40
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:10,040
And they're little handheld mirrors this morning as kind of an illustration.

41
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:16,200
But more than an illustration, I want you to spend some time and not rush through this.

42
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,720
But I want you to look at yourself.

43
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:21,800
But I would disciple you in this way.

44
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:26,860
Do not look at your face or your new wrinkles or how your hair looks.

45
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:31,380
But I want you to look at you as God sees you.

46
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:37,100
Spend some time really doing some self-reflection.

47
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:41,320
But self-reflection in the sense of your part.

48
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:44,280
What part do you play in the whole of the people of God?

49
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:48,880
This sermon is really geared at self-reflection.

50
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:56,600
I mean, Kent and Ryan have done a remarkable job at illustrating and inviting us into a story that doesn't just look down on a runaway prophet.

51
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,260
Jonah, I think, is a story that invites us to see ourselves.

52
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:07,160
Most of us know Jonah as the man who ran from God, got swallowed by a fish, and eventually went to Nineveh.

53
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:11,180
But if we're not careful, we'll keep the story of Jonah at a safe distance.

54
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:13,740
And this is my temptation, right?

55
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:20,280
We'll comfortably leave Jonah a character in our children's stories instead of letting him be what Scripture intends him to be.

56
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:22,200
A mirror.

57
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:28,480
Because Jonah shows us something uncomfortable about the human heart, about our heart.

58
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:31,840
Jonah knew that God was merciful.

59
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,760
That was not his confusion.

60
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:35,760
That was his complaint.

61
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:42,460
And Jonah did not run because he thought God would be too harsh or, like, kind of we've been reinforcing the idea.

62
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,560
I grew up in Sunday school that he was scared.

63
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:45,980
No.

64
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:53,160
Jonah ran because he knew that God was gracious, merciful, slow to anger, and abounding in steadfast love.

65
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:56,800
And Jonah was afraid that he might be that way towards his enemies as well.

66
00:04:57,940 --> 00:05:08,760
So before we ask, what was wrong with Jonah, I think a better reflective question is, where do I see Jonah in me?

67
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:15,280
Who do I secretly or publicly hope that God does not have mercy on?

68
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:19,000
Who would I rather see judged than restored?

69
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:30,500
Who are the people I can imagine God correcting, exposing, or punishing, but struggle to imagine God forgiving them?

70
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:36,860
That is the mirror today that Jonah holds up to all of our faces.

71
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:52,820
Thomas John Carlyle captures this uncomfortable tension in a poem called Addiction, where he imagines Jonah almost scolding God for being addicted to mercy.

72
00:05:52,980 --> 00:06:02,800
And I want you to read this, and I'm going to read it aloud, but I want you to ask yourselves sincerely if you've ever thought this way towards a group of people.

73
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:12,980
Consistently, Jonah chided his stupid and incredible creator for his addiction to mercy, as if it were some miracle drug.

74
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:20,000
A deity ought to be dependably capricious to keep the natives in line.

75
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:26,380
A G-bomb in an overpopulated slum would wipe out that delinquency in a hurry.

76
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:35,180
Naturally, Nineveh would make a perfect target once he, Jonah himself, was safely outside.

77
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:43,840
This church is the tension of Jonah.

78
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:48,100
What happens when God has mercy on the wrong people?

79
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,480
Not wrong to God, of course.

80
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:53,640
Wrong to Jonah.

81
00:06:53,640 --> 00:07:00,680
And maybe, and I'm hopeful and we're honest with ourselves this morning, wrong to us.

82
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:05,220
Stand with me as we hear the word of the Lord from Jonah chapter 3.

83
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:22,820
The word of the Lord came to Jonah a second time, get up and go to Nineveh, that great city, and declare against it the proclamation that I am commanding you.

84
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:27,540
And Jonah got up and went to Nineveh according to the words, the Lord's word.

85
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:31,700
Now Nineveh was indeed an enormous city, a three days walk across it.

86
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:37,940
Jonah started into the city walking one day and he cried out, just 40 more days and Nineveh will be overthrown.

87
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:42,060
And the people of Nineveh believed God.

88
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:48,200
They proclaimed a fast and put on mourning clothes from the greatest of them to the least significant.

89
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:57,820
When word of it reached the king of Nineveh, he got up from his throne, stripped himself of his robe, and covered himself with mourning clothes and sat in ashes.

90
00:07:58,820 --> 00:08:07,820
Then he announced in Nineveh, by decree of the king and his officials, neither human nor animal, cattle nor flock will taste anything, no grazing and no drinking water.

91
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:15,140
Let humans and animals alike put on mourning clothes and let them call upon God forcefully.

92
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:20,340
And let all persons stop their evil behavior and the violence that's under their control.

93
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:28,600
He thought, who knows, God may see this and turn from his wrath so that we may not perish.

94
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,740
God saw what they were doing, that they had ceased their evil behavior.

95
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:37,160
So God stopped planning to destroy them and he didn't do it.

96
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:38,680
This is the word of the Lord.

97
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:40,720
You guys can be seated.

98
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:48,000
I want to start that phrase, then the word of the Lord came to Jonah a second time.

99
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:57,120
I think that phrase matters because before Nineveh ever receives mercy in chapter three, Jonah, the prophet of God, receives mercy himself.

100
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:03,960
Before Jonah preaches to people, he does not think deserve another chance.

101
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:06,300
Jonah himself is given another chance.

102
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:11,340
And that means Jonah should not walk into Nineveh as a morally superior man.

103
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:15,000
He walks into Nineveh a rescued man.

104
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:18,840
He walks in as a man who has ran from God and lived.

105
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:22,640
He walks in as a man who has disobeyed but was not discarded.

106
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:29,740
He walks in as a man who went down to the sea, down into the belly of the fish, down to the depth, and yet God brought him back up.

107
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:35,880
Jonah is standing in chapter three because God has already been merciful to Jonah.

108
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:43,760
And that's important because sometimes the hardest people to convince of mercy are the people who have already received it.

109
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:46,260
Church, are you hearing this?

110
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:52,980
Because sometimes the hardest people to convince of God's mercy are the people who have already received it.

111
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:54,580
Isn't that a strange thought?

112
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:58,900
We would think that mercy would make us merciful people.

113
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:04,040
We would think forgiven people would be forgiving people.

114
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:12,420
We would think the people who have been spared the wrath of God would be eager to see others spared the wrath of God.

115
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:14,020
But that's not always what happens, is it?

116
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:20,920
Sometimes we receive mercy as though it's obvious that God would give me mercy.

117
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:26,040
Of course God should be patient with me.

118
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:30,040
Of course God would understand me.

119
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:33,120
Of course God should give me time.

120
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:36,880
Of course God knows that I'm complicated.

121
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:41,580
Of course God sees my whole story.

122
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:52,900
But then, if we're being honest, we look at someone else and suddenly we become very clear, very firm, very certain, very quick.

123
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:56,060
We want nuance for ourselves.

124
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:59,220
We want justice and judgment for them.

125
00:10:59,220 --> 00:11:04,160
We want restoration for ourselves, but God should expose their sins.

126
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:08,900
We want God to be gentle with us and severe with them.

127
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:13,860
This, folks, let me bring you into my theological brain.

128
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:16,680
This is what we might call Jonah syndrome.

129
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:26,200
Jonah syndrome is described as the spiritual sickness that gladly receives mercy from God while resenting when that same mercy reaches someone else.

130
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:33,220
Jonah syndrome says, I deserve mercy, but they deserve judgment.

131
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:37,780
And the first words of Jonah 3 already expose it.

132
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:41,040
The word of the Lord came to Jonah a second time.

133
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:46,700
Jonah is a second chance prophet to a last chance city.

134
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:51,260
And the scandal of that is, Jonah likes being a second chance prophet.

135
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:55,360
He just doesn't like going to a last chance city.

136
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:01,460
But God says, arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and call out the message that I tell you.

137
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:04,660
This is almost the same command that Jonah receives in chapter 1.

138
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:08,660
Right in Jonah chapter 1, Jonah runs.

139
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:11,080
Jonah chapter 3, he goes.

140
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:14,040
Now, we should be careful here.

141
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:19,700
Jonah obeys, but we should not assume that his heart has been completely transformed.

142
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:23,020
Jonah's feet are moving in the right direction.

143
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:28,540
But Jonah 4 will show us that Jonah's heart is still resisting the mercy of God.

144
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:30,980
And I think there's a word for us in there.

145
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:35,920
I think it's possible to obey God externally while still resenting God internally.

146
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:41,900
It is possible to do the right religious thing while still carrying bitterness in your heart.

147
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:48,760
It is possible to show up, serve, preach, sing, pray, and still be angry that God is more merciful than we are.

148
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:53,820
And church, I'm going to be honest, that's one of the reasons we need each other.

149
00:12:53,820 --> 00:13:02,000
Because you are probably better Christians than I am, but none of us can follow Jesus faithfully in isolation.

150
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,380
I am not a perfect Christian.

151
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:06,100
You are not a perfect Christian.

152
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:10,860
We are a people being formed together by the mercy of God.

153
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:17,040
And when we are left alone to our own justifications, Jonah syndrome quietly sneaks in.

154
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:19,980
We start baptizing our own resentment.

155
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:23,340
We start calling our bitterness discernment.

156
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:27,340
We start confusing our lack of mercy with holiness.

157
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:35,740
That is why we need the Spirit of God and the people of God to keep calling us back consistently to the way of Jesus.

158
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:39,120
Because I am not the bride of Christ myself.

159
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:43,700
We are the bride of Christ together.

160
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:47,640
And Jonah goes to Nineveh, but Jonah is not yet free.

161
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,640
And some of us know what that feels like.

162
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:54,880
We can be obedient and still bitter.

163
00:13:55,840 --> 00:14:00,320
We can be faithful in action and still need conversion and affection.

164
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:03,980
We can still be doing what God has asked.

165
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:07,160
But we need God to heal those desires.

166
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:12,220
Because the point of the story of Jonah is not simply do what God says.

167
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:14,300
That matters.

168
00:14:14,300 --> 00:14:16,560
But I think the book goes deeper.

169
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:20,980
The question is also, do you love what God loves?

170
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:27,760
Can your heart rejoice when the mercy of God reaches someone you would not have chosen?

171
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:30,780
That is the deeper question.

172
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:33,160
So Jonah goes.

173
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:36,420
The text says that Nineveh was an exceedingly great city.

174
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:43,560
It was large, significant, powerful, and from Israel's perspective, deeply threatening.

175
00:14:44,940 --> 00:14:49,940
See, I think we tend to theologize our way out of this, right?

176
00:14:49,940 --> 00:14:57,340
That Nineveh is just some random town with a bunch of neutral people that Jonah is called to evangelize.

177
00:14:57,340 --> 00:15:02,060
No.

178
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:12,420
Nineveh represented violence, empire, cruelty, oppression, and most of all, and I think in the forefront of Jonah's mind, enemy power.

179
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:21,520
So when God sends Jonah to Nineveh, God is sending him to the people he fears, the people he hates, and the people he believes deserve justice.

180
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:28,140
And Jonah walks into the city and preaches the most half-hearted sermon I have ever heard.

181
00:15:28,140 --> 00:15:33,040
Yet 40 days, and Nineveh will be overthrown.

182
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:35,420
That's it.

183
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:36,660
That's all you guys get.

184
00:15:36,660 --> 00:15:38,320
No poem.

185
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:40,980
No cool illustration.

186
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:44,420
No three points.

187
00:15:44,420 --> 00:15:46,300
No altar music.

188
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:47,740
No soft landing.

189
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:51,120
Just 40 days, and Nineveh will be overthrown.

190
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:57,160
I've spent hours writing and studying to give you a 35-ish minute sermon.

191
00:15:57,160 --> 00:16:03,520
Jonah gives the Ninevites, depending on your translation, seven to nine English words and five Hebrew words.

192
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:05,480
Five.

193
00:16:06,760 --> 00:16:10,220
It is one of the shortest sermons described in Scripture.

194
00:16:10,220 --> 00:16:18,960
And yet, it's what I want us to get in the mindset of, one of the most dramatic responses happens.

195
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:22,820
The people of Nineveh do not believe Jonah.

196
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:26,440
They believe the God of Israel.

197
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:28,300
That is what the text says.

198
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:30,740
Not merely, they did not believe Jonah.

199
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:32,300
They believed God.

200
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:37,300
It's a beautiful reminder that the power is not in Jonah's personality.

201
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:39,220
The power is not in Jonah's passion.

202
00:16:39,220 --> 00:16:43,160
The power is not in Jonah's compassion or lack thereof.

203
00:16:44,100 --> 00:16:47,020
The power is in the word of God.

204
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:52,480
The most reluctant sermon in the Bible sparks one of the most dramatic responses in the Bible.

205
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:54,660
Nineveh believes God.

206
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:56,880
They call for a fast.

207
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:02,820
They put on, I grew up in the King James, so I prefer sackcloth, but we saw morning clothes.

208
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:10,760
But the word reaches the king, and he rises from his throne, removes his robes, covers himself with sackcloth, and sits in ashes.

209
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:13,640
This is a stunning image.

210
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:18,620
The king of the enemy steps down.

211
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:21,140
The one seated above everyone else lowers himself.

212
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:23,460
The one dressed in royal robes takes them off.

213
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:28,380
And the one who sits in power now sits in ashes before a holy God.

214
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:32,500
He issues a decree that everyone should call out mightily to God.

215
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:38,320
Turn from evil and turn from the violence in their hands.

216
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:41,980
Stick on that phrase for a second.

217
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:45,040
The violence in their hands.

218
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:52,540
See, because I think, again, we as the Western church really like to theologize our way out of problems.

219
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:59,100
See, I think when we say repentance, we can be really vague about it.

220
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:00,040
Repent.

221
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:02,420
What does that look like?

222
00:18:02,420 --> 00:18:09,520
See, I think the beauty of Jonah chapter 3 is Nineveh's repentance is not vague.

223
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:11,860
It's not religious theater.

224
00:18:11,860 --> 00:18:13,680
It's not lip service.

225
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:14,480
It's not emotion.

226
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:16,960
It's not merely feeling bad about what they did.

227
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:22,440
They are called by their king to turn from evil and the violence in their hands.

228
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:28,440
Real repentance, folks, is not sorrow over consequences.

229
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:34,120
Real repentance is a turning away from the things that lead to death.

230
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,580
It is a turning from evil.

231
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:37,380
It is a turning from violence.

232
00:18:37,380 --> 00:18:40,860
It is a turning from the patterns that destroy our neighbors.

233
00:18:42,780 --> 00:18:47,040
It is a turning from the things that we have learned to justify.

234
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:52,380
Yeah.

235
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:59,440
See, real repentance turns.

236
00:19:00,260 --> 00:19:02,700
Then the king says, who knows?

237
00:19:02,700 --> 00:19:08,620
God may turn and relent and turn from his fierce anger so that we will not perish.

238
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:10,920
Who knows?

239
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:13,880
I don't think that's a presumption.

240
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:17,660
I think it's humility from the enemy.

241
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:22,240
The king does not say, God has to forgive us now because we've done the thing.

242
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:26,500
He does not say, we fasted, so now God owes us mercy.

243
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:29,880
No, he says, who knows?

244
00:19:29,880 --> 00:19:34,180
Maybe, maybe God will relent.

245
00:19:34,180 --> 00:19:36,780
Maybe God will show mercy.

246
00:19:36,780 --> 00:19:41,160
Maybe judgment is not the final word.

247
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:45,700
This is what repentance does.

248
00:19:45,700 --> 00:19:51,200
Repentance throws itself on the mercy of God without pretending to control the power of God.

249
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:56,480
Verse 10, when I saw what they did, how they turned their evil way,

250
00:19:56,480 --> 00:19:59,940
God relented of the disaster that he had said he would do to them,

251
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:01,640
and he did not do it.

252
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:02,980
There it is.

253
00:20:02,980 --> 00:20:06,540
The one thing Jonah feared would happen.

254
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:09,800
And if we're honest, the one thing we fear.

255
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:15,600
That God, the God of the universe and of all of creation would have mercy on our enemies.

256
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:19,020
That God would have mercy on Nineveh.

257
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:22,940
The wrong people, the violent people, the enemy people,

258
00:20:22,940 --> 00:20:29,280
the people Jonah wanted God to overthrow, crush, destroy with his wrath.

259
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:32,180
But here's the irony.

260
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:35,400
Nineveh is overthrown.

261
00:20:36,740 --> 00:20:39,000
Just not in the way Jonah expected it to be.

262
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:44,280
The city was turned upside down, but not by fire from heaven.

263
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:48,960
Not by a divine G-bomb dropped on an enemy city.

264
00:20:48,960 --> 00:20:53,240
Not by destruction while Jonah watched from a safe distance.

265
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:57,080
No, Nineveh was overthrown by repentance.

266
00:20:57,080 --> 00:21:00,560
Nineveh was overturned by mercy.

267
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:05,960
The city Jonah wanted God to flatten is instead turned upside down by grace.

268
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:10,020
And this is how the mercy of God works, folks.

269
00:21:10,020 --> 00:21:13,200
God's mercy does not ignore evil.

270
00:21:13,200 --> 00:21:17,220
No, I would argue God's mercy confronts evil where it lives.

271
00:21:17,220 --> 00:21:20,100
God's mercy names violence.

272
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:22,980
God's mercy calls for repentance.

273
00:21:22,980 --> 00:21:25,820
And God's mercy also tells the truth.

274
00:21:25,820 --> 00:21:32,440
But God's mercy also opens the door for a future beyond the worst thing we have ever done.

275
00:21:32,440 --> 00:21:38,400
And this is where we have to be careful because I think sometimes when we talk about mercy, people hear softness.

276
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:40,260
They hear avoidance.

277
00:21:40,260 --> 00:21:42,700
They hear pretending is not evil is not evil.

278
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:47,800
I had a conversation with a friend of mine this week about the idea that we're really good at drawing lines in the sand.

279
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:51,740
But we're not really good at our own repentance as people of God.

280
00:21:51,740 --> 00:21:54,980
But that is Jonah chapter 3.

281
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,800
God does not say that Nineveh's violence does not matter.

282
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:03,820
God sends a prophet to confront Nineveh's violence.

283
00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:08,620
God names the evil, warns of judgment, and God calls the city to turn.

284
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:13,020
See, mercy is not pretending sin is harmless.

285
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:15,800
Mercy is not making repentance.

286
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:18,720
Mercy is God making repentance possible.

287
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:24,040
Mercy, I believe, is God interrupting us on the road to destruction.

288
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:28,560
Mercy is God refusing to let evil have the final word.

289
00:22:28,560 --> 00:22:35,620
And Jonah, as a prophet of God, as a representative of God's people, he should be thrilled.

290
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:40,640
A whole city of my enemies have turned from violence.

291
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:43,320
A whole city humbles herself.

292
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:47,540
A whole city hears the warning and responds.

293
00:22:47,540 --> 00:22:53,100
We, as good Christian folk, we would rejoice.

294
00:22:53,100 --> 00:22:54,360
Right?

295
00:22:56,880 --> 00:22:58,620
You would think Jonah would rejoice.

296
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:01,820
You would think that Jonah would worship.

297
00:23:01,820 --> 00:23:10,540
You would think Jonah would say, praise God, the mercy that saved me from the depths of the sea has now saved Nineveh from her destruction.

298
00:23:13,340 --> 00:23:14,260
We'll steal some Orion's sermon now.

299
00:23:14,260 --> 00:23:15,140
We'll steal some Orion's sermon now.

300
00:23:15,140 --> 00:23:16,600
It's not what happens.

301
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:22,640
Because I believe Jonah syndrome does not rejoice when enemies repent.

302
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:25,340
No, Jonah syndrome is suspicious.

303
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:28,520
Jonah syndrome says, hmm, we'll see if it lasts.

304
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:33,880
Jonah syndrome says, they're only sorry because they got caught.

305
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:40,000
Jonah syndrome says, people like that don't deserve another chance.

306
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:44,300
Jonah syndrome says, God, I know you forgave me.

307
00:23:44,300 --> 00:23:46,200
But this feels different.

308
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:51,620
And I think this is where the sermon starts to press in on us.

309
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:54,800
Because mercy for me feels like grace.

310
00:23:54,800 --> 00:23:58,520
But mercy for them feels like injustice.

311
00:24:02,760 --> 00:24:05,840
Jonah 3 ends with God relenting from disaster.

312
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:10,760
But Jonah 4 begins a story about how Jonah is angry about it.

313
00:24:10,760 --> 00:24:15,580
That means the miracle of Jonah 3 becomes the crisis of Jonah 4.

314
00:24:15,580 --> 00:24:17,420
Here's my three points.

315
00:24:17,420 --> 00:24:21,680
In our words, Nineveh repents.

316
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:23,440
God relents.

317
00:24:23,440 --> 00:24:25,520
Jonah resents.

318
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:28,060
Way better than Jonah.

319
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:32,000
And I think that tells us something important.

320
00:24:32,760 --> 00:24:36,220
The book of Jonah is not only about getting Nineveh's heart right.

321
00:24:36,220 --> 00:24:37,740
I think we can focus on that, right?

322
00:24:37,740 --> 00:24:39,380
Nineveh needed to repent.

323
00:24:39,380 --> 00:24:47,180
But I think the thrust of the story is also Jonah, God's representative, also needed to repent.

324
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:50,220
Because Jonah is a prophet of Israel.

325
00:24:50,220 --> 00:24:52,780
Jonah becomes a mirror for us as people of God.

326
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:57,860
God is not only pursuing a pagan city.

327
00:24:57,860 --> 00:24:59,500
He is pursuing the prophet.

328
00:24:59,500 --> 00:25:02,020
He is pursuing his people.

329
00:25:02,760 --> 00:25:07,740
God is not only dealing with Nineveh's violence, but he's also dealing with Jonah's bitterness.

330
00:25:07,740 --> 00:25:12,440
And this is kind of the point Ryan was making this morning.

331
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:15,240
God is not just trying to save them.

332
00:25:15,240 --> 00:25:17,980
He's also trying to save us.

333
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:25,260
God is trying to save Jonah from the kind of heart that cannot rejoice in mercy.

334
00:25:25,260 --> 00:25:30,420
And maybe that's why this little book is so powerful.

335
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:35,140
Because most of us, I think, if we're honest with ourselves, know what it is to be Jonah.

336
00:25:35,140 --> 00:25:40,540
We know what it is to want patience for ourselves and God's punishment for someone else.

337
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:45,720
We know what it is to enjoy being forgiven, yet struggle to forgive.

338
00:25:45,720 --> 00:25:52,160
We know what it is to sing amazing grace while secretly believing some people are beyond it.

339
00:25:53,760 --> 00:25:59,380
And so the question that comes to us, who is Nineveh to us?

340
00:25:59,380 --> 00:26:02,020
Maybe it's personal.

341
00:26:02,020 --> 00:26:04,520
Maybe it's someone who hurt you.

342
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:07,100
Maybe it's someone who betrayed you.

343
00:26:07,100 --> 00:26:09,840
Maybe it's someone who walked away.

344
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:11,580
Someone who lied.

345
00:26:11,580 --> 00:26:13,380
Someone who damaged your trust.

346
00:26:14,540 --> 00:26:16,840
Someone whose name still makes you get a knot in your stomach.

347
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:21,660
And I want to be careful here because mercy does not mean pretending the wounds were not real.

348
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:26,000
Mercy does not mean you have to trust someone who has become untrustworthy.

349
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:29,000
Mercy does not mean there are no boundaries.

350
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,700
Mercy does not erase justice.

351
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:36,820
It restores what evil has broken.

352
00:26:36,820 --> 00:26:43,160
But Jonah 3 still asks us whether or not we actually want redemption for the people who have done us wrong.

353
00:26:44,100 --> 00:26:48,480
Or whether deep down, inside, if we're honest with ourselves, we only want them punished.

354
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,660
Maybe Nineveh is corporate.

355
00:26:51,660 --> 00:26:55,200
Maybe it's just not someone who hurt me, but it's someone who hurt us.

356
00:26:55,200 --> 00:27:02,560
Maybe it's a group that we have learned to fear, resent, or despise.

357
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:09,720
Maybe it's people who we talk more about enemies to be erased than image bearers who need repentance.

358
00:27:11,580 --> 00:27:16,040
Maybe it's people whose suffering feels acceptable to us because they were born on the wrong side of the line.

359
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:27,080
Maybe Jonah syndrome shows up when our first instinct is not prayer, not grief, not hope for repentance, but contempt.

360
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:30,320
Maybe Nineveh is cultural.

361
00:27:31,780 --> 00:27:35,880
Maybe it's people whose lifestyle, worldview, language, or values feel threatening to us.

362
00:27:35,880 --> 00:27:39,860
Maybe it's people we've reduced to a category.

363
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:47,720
These people, that crowd, are simply them.

364
00:27:49,660 --> 00:28:00,160
And once we reduce people, humans made in the image of God to a category, it becomes easier to imagine judgment for them, but mercy for us.

365
00:28:00,160 --> 00:28:03,320
Maybe Nineveh is religious.

366
00:28:03,320 --> 00:28:05,640
I'm going to step on some toes here.

367
00:28:05,640 --> 00:28:09,020
Maybe it's the people who are convinced the others are doing faith wrong.

368
00:28:10,460 --> 00:28:16,040
It's the people who worship differently, vote differently, read differently, think differently.

369
00:28:16,040 --> 00:28:20,280
The people we suspect that God would agree with us about.

370
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:27,380
And then Jonah 3 asked the question, what if God's mercy is bigger than our imagination?

371
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:32,600
What if God is already moving towards people we have written off?

372
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:38,320
What if the spirit of God is already at work at the place we would rather avoid?

373
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:44,460
What if God truly intends to make our enemy our brother?

374
00:28:44,460 --> 00:28:50,120
What if God has mercy on the wrong people?

375
00:28:50,120 --> 00:28:55,080
This is the scandal of the gospel.

376
00:28:55,080 --> 00:28:57,340
This is the good news.

377
00:28:57,340 --> 00:29:02,820
Because the gospel announces that none of us are standing here today because we are the right people.

378
00:29:02,820 --> 00:29:08,200
We are standing here as second chance prophets because God is merciful.

379
00:29:09,140 --> 00:29:11,080
We are not deserving people.

380
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:13,520
We are rescued people.

381
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:15,700
We are not people who have earned grace.

382
00:29:15,700 --> 00:29:17,340
We are people that grace has found.

383
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:24,240
And once that becomes clear, it becomes harder to privatize our faith and our list of people that God should not pursue.

384
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:31,200
Because at the center of our faith is Jesus Christ who does not merely announce mercy from a distance.

385
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:32,740
He embodied it.

386
00:29:32,740 --> 00:29:34,680
He comes to our enemies.

387
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:35,960
He eats with sinners.

388
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:37,220
He touches the unclean.

389
00:29:37,220 --> 00:29:39,060
He forgives the guilty.

390
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:41,400
He prays for his executioners.

391
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:45,980
Jesus bears the violence of this world without returning it.

392
00:29:45,980 --> 00:29:53,260
And at the cross, we see the truth about human sin and divine mercy at the same time.

393
00:29:54,160 --> 00:29:58,980
The cross should tell us our sin is worse than we think it is.

394
00:29:58,980 --> 00:30:04,120
But it should also tell us that God's mercy is greater than we ever imagined.

395
00:30:04,120 --> 00:30:09,860
The resurrection tells us the judgment and death do not get the final word in God's world.

396
00:30:09,860 --> 00:30:11,580
Jesus does.

397
00:30:12,940 --> 00:30:16,980
So when we ask who deserves mercy, the gospel actually gives us a better question.

398
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:21,760
Who has God chosen to pursue with mercy?

399
00:30:21,760 --> 00:30:25,380
And how might I partner with God to deliver that message?

400
00:30:26,980 --> 00:30:31,080
The answer is more uncomfortable than we might want to admit.

401
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:33,660
Because God pursued Jonah.

402
00:30:33,660 --> 00:30:35,660
God pursued Nineveh.

403
00:30:35,660 --> 00:30:38,220
Thank God God pursues us.

404
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:41,480
Not because we're always right.

405
00:30:41,480 --> 00:30:45,620
Not because our anger is righteous.

406
00:30:47,820 --> 00:30:49,660
Not because our instincts are holy.

407
00:30:49,660 --> 00:30:56,740
But because God is gracious and merciful and slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love.

408
00:30:56,740 --> 00:31:00,340
This is the God Jonah resented.

409
00:31:00,340 --> 00:31:04,720
This is the God Nineveh needed.

410
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:07,700
This is the God we worship.

411
00:31:10,140 --> 00:31:13,520
And I think this is a point where Jonah points us to Jesus.

412
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:14,480
Because you know me.

413
00:31:14,480 --> 00:31:15,820
I always like to get it back to Jesus.

414
00:31:15,820 --> 00:31:18,940
There's a moment in John's gospel.

415
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:19,960
In John 12.

416
00:31:19,960 --> 00:31:21,800
Where some Greeks, some Gentiles come and say,

417
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:24,360
Sir, we've heard about this Jesus.

418
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:25,880
We want to see Jesus.

419
00:31:25,880 --> 00:31:29,440
And throughout John's gospel, Jesus has consistently been saying,

420
00:31:29,440 --> 00:31:31,300
My hour has not yet come.

421
00:31:31,300 --> 00:31:32,840
The wedding at Cana.

422
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:34,700
My hour has not yet come.

423
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:37,800
At other moments of conflict and expectation.

424
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:39,600
My hour has not yet come.

425
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:45,100
But in John 12, when we see Gentiles, the others looking for him,

426
00:31:45,100 --> 00:31:45,840
Jesus says,

427
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:49,240
The hour has come for the Son of Man to be glorified.

428
00:31:49,240 --> 00:31:52,000
And I think that should stop us in our tracks.

429
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:59,300
Because when the nations, the outsiders, them, start coming to Jesus,

430
00:31:59,300 --> 00:32:02,240
Jesus knows his hour has arrived.

431
00:32:02,240 --> 00:32:06,280
Because the mercy of God is ever moving outward.

432
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:09,060
The mercy of God is crossing boundaries

433
00:32:09,060 --> 00:32:12,840
The mercy of God is reaching beyond the expected people,

434
00:32:12,840 --> 00:32:14,420
Beyond the insiders,

435
00:32:14,420 --> 00:32:16,740
Beyond the comfortable religious lines.

436
00:32:16,740 --> 00:32:18,420
And almost immediately,

437
00:32:18,420 --> 00:32:20,080
When that happens,

438
00:32:20,080 --> 00:32:21,000
Jesus says,

439
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,120
They're going to kill me.

440
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:28,440
The grain of wheat must fall into the earth and die.

441
00:32:29,520 --> 00:32:30,660
And I think there's something deeply important there.

442
00:32:30,660 --> 00:32:31,840
And I think there's something deeply important there.

443
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:35,220
Because Jesus knew what Jonah did not want to admit.

444
00:32:35,220 --> 00:32:38,700
That God's mercy was never supposed to stop at one people.

445
00:32:38,700 --> 00:32:42,260
God's mercy was never meant to be locked inside one nation.

446
00:32:42,260 --> 00:32:45,440
God's mercy was always moving towards the world,

447
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:47,520
Towards the whole of his creation.

448
00:32:48,160 --> 00:32:50,540
But when mercy moves towards the wrong people,

449
00:32:50,540 --> 00:32:53,940
The people in power often feel the most threatened.

450
00:32:53,940 --> 00:32:58,080
When Gentiles, those outside, come to Jesus,

451
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:01,400
The national and religious imaginations of the leaders cannot handle it.

452
00:33:01,900 --> 00:33:04,340
And the question for us becomes even more painful.

453
00:33:04,340 --> 00:33:06,500
If this is your first week, I apologize.

454
00:33:06,500 --> 00:33:12,120
Have we ever become so protective of our own interest,

455
00:33:12,120 --> 00:33:13,060
Of our own group,

456
00:33:13,060 --> 00:33:14,080
Of our own nation,

457
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:15,140
Of our own side,

458
00:33:15,140 --> 00:33:17,500
On our own sense of who deserves mercy,

459
00:33:17,500 --> 00:33:20,020
That we would rather silence the authority of Jesus

460
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:21,380
Than follow him into that mercy?

461
00:33:23,440 --> 00:33:26,720
Have we become so committed to protecting what we call ours,

462
00:33:26,720 --> 00:33:29,340
That we resist the wideness of the kingdom of God?

463
00:33:29,340 --> 00:33:33,100
Have we become so nationalistic,

464
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:33,700
So tribal,

465
00:33:33,700 --> 00:33:36,560
So certain that God agrees with us in our boundaries,

466
00:33:36,560 --> 00:33:39,320
That we crucify the voice of Christ in our lives,

467
00:33:39,320 --> 00:33:41,160
When he tells us to love our enemies,

468
00:33:41,160 --> 00:33:43,160
Pray for those who persecute us,

469
00:33:43,160 --> 00:33:45,220
And rejoice when mercy reaches people,

470
00:33:45,220 --> 00:33:46,780
We would not have chosen.

471
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:51,280
Because that is the mirror we hold in front of our faces.

472
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:56,040
Jonah, like us,

473
00:33:56,040 --> 00:33:59,500
Wanted a God whose mercy stopped where my mercy stops.

474
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:05,820
But Jesus reveals a God whose mercy keeps moving outward.

475
00:34:05,820 --> 00:34:08,000
Towards Israel,

476
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:09,400
Towards the nations,

477
00:34:09,400 --> 00:34:10,660
Towards sinners,

478
00:34:10,660 --> 00:34:11,920
Towards enemies,

479
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:13,920
And thank God towards us,

480
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:17,600
And towards the people we still struggle to love.

481
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:20,900
Thomas John Carlyle has written another poem,

482
00:34:20,900 --> 00:34:22,040
And I won't put it up on the screen,

483
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:23,580
But it's called The Great Intruder.

484
00:34:23,580 --> 00:34:26,680
He describes how exhausting it is,

485
00:34:26,680 --> 00:34:28,960
To be so persistently called,

486
00:34:28,960 --> 00:34:31,020
When the last thing we want to do is get up and go.

487
00:34:31,020 --> 00:34:34,260
And he has this line in the poem,

488
00:34:34,260 --> 00:34:37,000
He says that God keeps haunting us,

489
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:38,740
Like some Holy Ghost.

490
00:34:38,740 --> 00:34:42,800
For all my liturgical calendar people out there,

491
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:44,760
What a great reminder for us,

492
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:45,640
On the seventh day,

493
00:34:45,640 --> 00:34:47,460
At seventh Sunday after Easter,

494
00:34:47,460 --> 00:34:50,100
Where we find ourselves in the great in between,

495
00:34:50,100 --> 00:34:53,280
When the church calendar between Ascension Day and Pentecost.

496
00:34:53,280 --> 00:34:56,960
The church of Acts is waiting to be sent beyond themselves,

497
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:58,320
From Jerusalem to Judea,

498
00:34:58,320 --> 00:34:59,120
To Samaria,

499
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:00,660
To the ends of the earth.

500
00:35:00,660 --> 00:35:01,500
In other words,

501
00:35:01,500 --> 00:35:05,740
The Holy Spirit does not come to comfort us where we are.

502
00:35:07,520 --> 00:35:12,820
The Spirit comes to send the church into all the world in the authority of the risen Christ.

503
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:14,700
And that,

504
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:15,240
Folks,

505
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:17,700
Is exactly where Jonah exposes us.

506
00:35:17,700 --> 00:35:19,640
Because sometimes,

507
00:35:19,640 --> 00:35:22,200
Maybe even more than oftentimes,

508
00:35:22,200 --> 00:35:24,600
We want the Spirit's comfort

509
00:35:24,600 --> 00:35:27,640
Without the Spirit's interruption of our lives.

510
00:35:27,640 --> 00:35:31,220
We want Pentecostal power

511
00:35:31,220 --> 00:35:33,800
Without Pentecostal mission.

512
00:35:33,800 --> 00:35:36,600
We want to fill this room.

513
00:35:37,540 --> 00:35:39,700
We want God to fill this room,

514
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:44,040
But we don't want Him to walk us towards Nineveh.

515
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:45,720
Or Jonah.

516
00:35:45,720 --> 00:35:47,420
This is us.

517
00:35:47,420 --> 00:35:51,080
But thank God that God keeps intruding.

518
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:53,720
God intrudes on Jonah's escape.

519
00:35:53,720 --> 00:35:55,440
God intrudes on Jonah's storm.

520
00:35:55,440 --> 00:35:58,360
God intrudes on Jonah's planned drowning.

521
00:35:58,360 --> 00:36:01,120
God intrudes on Jonah's bitterness.

522
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:04,000
And He intrudes on Nineveh's violence.

523
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:06,940
God intrudes on us.

524
00:36:06,940 --> 00:36:11,800
He intrudes on the places we have confused our resentment for righteousness.

525
00:36:11,800 --> 00:36:15,620
He intrudes on the places we have confused our boundaries with holiness.

526
00:36:15,620 --> 00:36:19,980
He intrudes on the places we have confused our political instincts with the kingdom of God.

527
00:36:19,980 --> 00:36:25,480
He intrudes on the places where we have received mercy but refuse to become merciful.

528
00:36:25,480 --> 00:36:28,440
He intrudes on our Jonah syndrome.

529
00:36:29,640 --> 00:36:34,660
And that intrusion is grace and mercy even for us.

530
00:36:34,660 --> 00:36:40,100
Because God loves us too much to remain a people who only celebrate mercy when it benefits us.

531
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:47,540
God loves us too much to let us worship a merciful Savior while secretly despising that same mercy for others.

532
00:36:47,540 --> 00:36:53,080
God loves us too much to let our hearts stay hard and small.

533
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:56,940
So Jonah 3 leaves us with a question.

534
00:36:58,580 --> 00:37:00,600
Not simply will Nineveh repent.

535
00:37:00,600 --> 00:37:03,160
Because Nineveh does repent.

536
00:37:03,160 --> 00:37:05,720
Not simply will God show mercy.

537
00:37:05,720 --> 00:37:08,600
Because God does show mercy.

538
00:37:08,600 --> 00:37:13,620
The question is, can Jonah rejoice?

539
00:37:13,620 --> 00:37:17,700
Can Jonah rejoice when God has mercy on the wrong people?

540
00:37:19,480 --> 00:37:21,420
So the question then becomes, can we?

541
00:37:21,420 --> 00:37:26,200
Can we rejoice when God gives someone else a second chance?

542
00:37:26,200 --> 00:37:29,700
Can we rejoice when someone we dislike begins to change?

543
00:37:29,700 --> 00:37:33,740
Can we rejoice when mercy reaches across the lines we've drawn?

544
00:37:34,740 --> 00:37:39,420
Can we rejoice when grace that saved us refuses to stop with us?

545
00:37:39,540 --> 00:37:43,680
And maybe today, the invitation is twofold.

546
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:47,000
For some of us, I think the invitation is to repent.

547
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,800
To come honestly before God.

548
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:54,780
And say, Lord, I have wanted mercy for myself and judgment for someone else.

549
00:37:55,900 --> 00:37:58,700
I have carried contempt and called it conviction.

550
00:37:58,700 --> 00:38:01,540
I have confused bitterness with discernment.

551
00:38:01,540 --> 00:38:07,220
I have reduced people made in your image to categories, enemies, threats, or problems to be solved.

552
00:38:08,360 --> 00:38:12,660
I have loved the idea of grace until it starts moving to people I do not think deserve.

553
00:38:12,660 --> 00:38:17,500
Lord, I want you to save me from my own Jonah syndrome.

554
00:38:17,500 --> 00:38:20,900
Give me the heart of Jesus who I claim to follow.

555
00:38:20,900 --> 00:38:26,200
And track with me here because this is a very simple idea and we've lost the plot.

556
00:38:26,200 --> 00:38:30,700
If we claim to know Jesus the same as the original disciples,

557
00:38:30,700 --> 00:38:36,860
then our end goal is to grow and mature into the kind of people who accurately represent Jesus.

558
00:38:38,360 --> 00:38:44,380
For others of us, maybe the invitation is simply to come home to mercy.

559
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:48,260
Maybe you hear this sermon and you don't feel like Jonah at all.

560
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:52,540
You feel like Nineveh inside the city.

561
00:38:52,540 --> 00:38:57,540
You know there are things on your hands that need to be surrendered and repented of.

562
00:38:57,540 --> 00:38:59,860
You know these patterns deeply.

563
00:38:59,860 --> 00:39:06,040
Sin, wounds, habits, violence that needs to be brought to the mercy of God.

564
00:39:07,460 --> 00:39:09,280
Hear and receive the good news.

565
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:11,320
God's mercy does not ignore evil.

566
00:39:11,320 --> 00:39:13,820
God's mercy confronts it.

567
00:39:13,820 --> 00:39:20,840
God's mercy also wants to open the door for a future beyond the worst thing you have done.

568
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:26,840
We sang today, I was made to be more than tending a grave.

569
00:39:26,840 --> 00:39:28,280
Do we believe it?

570
00:39:28,840 --> 00:39:33,540
The book of Romans says, but God demonstrated his own love for us in this.

571
00:39:33,540 --> 00:39:37,120
While we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.

572
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:39,280
Not after we've cleaned ourselves up.

573
00:39:39,280 --> 00:39:41,480
Not after we became that right kind of people.

574
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:44,640
Not after we proved we were worthy to follow Jesus.

575
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:46,720
No, while we were yet sinners.

576
00:39:46,720 --> 00:39:48,820
And Paul goes even further.

577
00:39:48,820 --> 00:39:54,400
While we were yet enemies of God, we were reconciled to him through the death of his son.

578
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:59,060
That means mercy of God is not waiting for you at the finish line when you die.

579
00:39:59,060 --> 00:40:01,880
The mercy of God meets you here and now.

580
00:40:01,880 --> 00:40:04,700
It meets you in the belly of the fish.

581
00:40:04,700 --> 00:40:08,240
It meets you while you live in a city full of violence.

582
00:40:08,380 --> 00:40:10,340
It meets you in the mirror.

583
00:40:10,340 --> 00:40:14,720
It meets you most personally in the person of Jesus.

584
00:40:14,720 --> 00:40:17,960
So I would say, come and repent.

585
00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:20,300
Come and receive mercy.

586
00:40:20,300 --> 00:40:26,020
Come and see the good creation and be a part of new creation that Christ has invited us into.

587
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:32,040
Come and find that the father's arms are far more wider than you ever imagined.

588
00:40:33,580 --> 00:40:36,400
The good news is that not that God had mercy on Nineveh.

589
00:40:36,400 --> 00:40:41,140
The good news is that God had mercy on Jonah too.

590
00:40:41,140 --> 00:40:45,120
And that means there is mercy for people even like us.

591
00:40:45,120 --> 00:40:49,480
People who have received grace and still struggle when grace goes somewhere else.

592
00:40:49,480 --> 00:40:54,820
People who know the right things about God, but still need our hearts converted to the mercy of God.

593
00:40:54,820 --> 00:40:58,540
People who want God to be merciful, but not too merciful.

594
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:03,320
Now, normally this would be the kind of Sunday where we come to the communion table together.

595
00:41:03,320 --> 00:41:07,900
But this morning, we're intentionally not taking communion.

596
00:41:07,900 --> 00:41:11,300
Not because the table is absent from this message.

597
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:14,600
But because the table, I think, is pressing in on us.

598
00:41:14,600 --> 00:41:17,700
This morning, Jonah 3 has placed a mirror in front of us.

599
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:23,320
So what I want you to do is do some honest work.

600
00:41:23,320 --> 00:41:26,040
Let the spirit do some honest work.

601
00:41:26,040 --> 00:41:28,160
Let it do some searching in us.

602
00:41:29,380 --> 00:41:33,980
Because next week, Ryan's going to lead us into Jonah 4 or Peyton, depending.

603
00:41:33,980 --> 00:41:39,100
Where we'll be confronted again with the wideness of God's mercy.

604
00:41:39,100 --> 00:41:41,020
And in a sense, the communion table of God.

605
00:41:41,020 --> 00:41:44,680
A table where mercy is not only offered to me.

606
00:41:44,680 --> 00:41:50,260
A table where grace is not only offered to people like us.

607
00:41:50,260 --> 00:41:54,760
A table where Jesus gathers people we would have never gathered.

608
00:41:56,600 --> 00:42:02,600
A table if we believe that God is faithful, that our enemies become family.

609
00:42:02,680 --> 00:42:08,300
A table where the Father's mercy is wider than Jonah wanted to imagine.

610
00:42:08,300 --> 00:42:10,900
And often wider than we ever imagined.

611
00:42:10,900 --> 00:42:14,320
So today, Marissa's going to come and sing.

612
00:42:14,320 --> 00:42:16,300
And I invite you not to rush past the mirror.

613
00:42:16,300 --> 00:42:20,540
Have the hard internal conversation with God.

614
00:42:20,540 --> 00:42:22,740
And where the spirit leads.

615
00:42:23,140 --> 00:42:25,280
Maybe even have the hard external conversation.

616
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:27,620
Let the spirit ask the question.

617
00:42:27,620 --> 00:42:30,240
Who is Nineveh to me?

618
00:42:30,240 --> 00:42:34,360
Who have I secretly hoped God would not have mercy on?

619
00:42:34,360 --> 00:42:38,340
Where have I received mercy but refused to become merciful?

620
00:42:38,340 --> 00:42:41,880
Where is Jesus inviting me to repent?

621
00:42:42,960 --> 00:42:44,600
Where is Jesus inviting me to come home?

622
00:42:44,600 --> 00:42:51,060
So God can save us from this Jonah syndrome that has creeped into our lives.

623
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:53,760
May God make us honest about our Nineveh.

624
00:42:53,760 --> 00:42:56,720
May God widen our mercy as a church.

625
00:42:56,720 --> 00:43:00,060
May God teach us to rejoice when sinners repent.

626
00:43:00,060 --> 00:43:01,200
When enemies are restored.

627
00:43:01,200 --> 00:43:02,460
When violent people turn.

628
00:43:02,460 --> 00:43:04,960
When proud people humble themselves.

629
00:43:04,960 --> 00:43:07,180
And when grace reaches people we'd never expected.

630
00:43:08,280 --> 00:43:11,520
Because Jonah thought that God's mercy was the problem.

631
00:43:11,520 --> 00:43:15,780
But Jonah's mercy was the thing that actually needed saving.

632
00:43:15,780 --> 00:43:18,380
Maybe ours is too.

633
00:43:18,380 --> 00:43:23,140
As Marissa sings, I encourage you to listen.

634
00:43:23,140 --> 00:43:26,760
And let this song do some gentle holy work in the hardest parts of your heart.

635
00:43:26,760 --> 00:43:29,960
If you need prayer, we're here.

636
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:30,760
I'm here.

637
00:43:30,760 --> 00:43:31,500
I'll sit up front.

638
00:43:31,500 --> 00:43:35,500
If you need someone to help you discern what repentance looks like, we're here.

639
00:43:36,540 --> 00:43:40,820
If you need to come home to mercy, the Father is not reluctant.

640
00:43:40,820 --> 00:43:48,040
Come and be a part of the mercy-filled miracle that God wants to do in you and in us.
